<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sublime Porte</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sublime Porte was the name given to the central government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, after the gate leading to the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>'s offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire describe it as the seat of administration. Foreign powers conducted diplomacy with the Porte.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sublime-porte.jpg" alt="Sublime Porte" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bab-i_Ali.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name goes back to an old practice in which a ruler announced his decisions and judgements at the gate of his palace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> followed this custom, and the Ottoman sultans adopted it from the time of Orhan I. The palace of the sultan, or the gate leading to it, therefore came to be called the High Gate. The name referred first to a palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the gate now known as the Imperial Gate, which led to the outermost courtyard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, became known as the High Gate or Sublime Porte.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When Sultan Suleiman sealed an alliance with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> in 1536, the French diplomats walked through the monumental gate, then called Bâb-ı Âlî, to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">Vizierate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the seat of the sultan's government. French was the language of diplomacy, and its translation, Sublime Porte, was soon adopted in most other European languages, English among them. It was used not only for the gate but as a metonymy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p>In the 18th century a large Italian-styled office building was built just west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a> area, on the other side of Alemdar Caddesi. It became the location of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> and many ministries, and from then on this building and the gate leading to its courtyards bore the name Sublime Porte. Colloquially it was also called the Gate of the Pasha. The building was rebuilt after a fire in 1839 and badly damaged by another fire in 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The building housed the office of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, the Ministry of the Interior and the Supreme Council of Judicial Ordinances. Western states used the term in diplomacy because their envoys were received at the porte, the Turkish word for which means gate.</p><p>After the Young Turk Revolution and the start of the Second Constitutional Era in 1908, the reformed Imperial Government replaced the functions of the classical Divan-ı Hümayun. In this period the word porte came to refer to the Foreign Ministry, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>'s office came to resemble that of a prime minister, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> became members of his cabinet as government ministers. The buildings now house the office of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> governor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sublime Porte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Sublime Porte</b>, the grand vizier’s administration, managed a bureaucracy that impressed European observers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman suzerainty, accepted with tribute from 1456 and tightened after 1538, meant that princes were confirmed and increasingly appointed by the <b>Porte</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1838 the <b>Sublime Porte</b> signed the Treaty of Balta Liman with Britain, which dismantled Ottoman trade monopolies and flooded the markets with European goods.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1912 coup, the Balkan Wars of 1912–1913 and the 1913 Raid on the <b>Sublime Porte</b>, a radicalized CUP took over.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>As a compromise the Ottoman Sultan made it an autonomous principality, established in 1834, with a Christian prince appointed by the <b>Porte</b> and paying an annual tribute.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-samos">Principality of Samos</a></li></ul></section>
